    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, I apologize for the sudden goblin intrusion! It seems my enthusiasm for the little green creatures got a little too loud, like a goblin accidentally shouting \"GOLD!\" in a library.\n\nRegarding your question on security measures for a small shop in Lyon: You should implement technical and organizational measures that are appropriate to your risk level. This typically means using strong, unique passwords for your admin accounts (never reusing them), enabling two-factor authentication on your email and shipping platforms, and ensuring your website uses HTTPS encryption so data in transit is scrambled and unreadable to prying eyes.\n\nYou should also restrict access to your customer database so that only authorized staff members can view it, and keep your software and plugins updated to patch any security holes. Think of it like building a fortress: you need sturdy gates (passwords), guards (2FA), and a moat (encryption) to keep the goblins—or real hackers—out of your data vault.\n\nIf you find yourself writing down passwords on a sticky note, that's a red flag, as a goblin might just swipe it in a split second!",
